<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammad Hatta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammad Hatta was an Indonesian nationalist leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> he cooperated with the Japanese occupation to advance independence and signed the proclamation of 17 August 1945. He became the first vice-president, led the delegation that secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> recognition in 1949 and was prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. He resigned the vice-presidency in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta.jpg" alt="Mohammad Hatta" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Written by St. Rais Alamsjah, published by Mutiara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drs._Mohammad_Hatta,_10_Orang_Indonesia_Terbesar_Sekarang_(1952),_p30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
